#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# unilateral_convolve

#% text
# `unilateral_convolve(f, g, tau, t)` gives the convolution of `f` and `g` on `[0, inf)`: the integral of `f(tau) g(t - tau)` for `tau` from 0 to `t`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `t` is taken positive; `assumptions: c` adds the condition `c`.

#% item
# Factors that do not depend on `tau` come out of the convolution, and a `dirac_delta(tau)` factor gives the other function at `t`.

#% item
# `unilateral_convolve(f, g, [tau1, ...], [t1, ...])` convolves in several variables.

#% item
# When the integral cannot be done the call stays unevaluated.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
unilateral_convolve(exp(-tau), exp(-2*tau), tau, t)
#% output
#= (-1 + exp(t))*exp(-2*t)

#% cell
unilateral_convolve(tau, exp(tau), tau, t)
#% output
#= -1 - t + exp(t)

#% cell
unilateral_convolve(dirac_delta(tau), f(tau), tau, t)
#% output
#= f(t)

#% cell
unilateral_convolve(a*r(tau), s(tau), tau, t)
#% output
#= a*unilateral_convolve(r(tau), s(tau), tau, t)

#% cell
unilateral_convolve(exp(-2*tau), tau*sin(tau), tau, t)
#% output
#= (1/25)*(-4*exp(-2*t) - 3*sin(t) + 4*cos(t) - 5*t*cos(t) + 10*t*sin(t))

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `mellin_convolve`, `laplace_transform`, `integrate`
